devon_customer: Hey team — running into a tool-calling failure when we mirror OpenAI's function call flow. Model keeps returning invalid enums and sometimes the args are stringified. Example payload we send:

```json
POST /v1/chat/completions
{
  "model": "open-compat-1",
  "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"Get weather for San Francisco"}],
  "functions": [
    {"name": "getWeather", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"city": {"type":"string"}, "unit": {"type":"string", "enum": ["metric", "imperial"]}}, "required": ["city"]}}
  ]
}
```

devon_customer: Model response (what we saw):

```json
{
  "id": "c-xxx",
  "choices": [
    {
      "finish_reason": "function_call",
      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": null,
        "function_call": {
          "name": "GetWeather",   // note capitalized/alternate name
          "arguments": "{\"city\":\"San Francisco\",\"unit\":\"celsius\"}" // stringified JSON and non-enum value
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

devon_customer: Our server validates function_call.arguments against the declared schema and throws 400: "Expected object for arguments but received string". Also when we try to accept string we still fail because unit "celsius" isn't in the enum.

maria_support: Thanks for the clear repro, Devon. We see two separate incompatibilities here: 1) function_call.arguments as a string vs object, 2) enum aliasing (celsius vs metric). Both are common when using models not perfectly aligned with the OpenAI function schema.

ash_engineer: Quick questions: which model family exactly (open-compat-1)? Any system or temperature overrides? Also do you pass a "function_call": "auto" or force a particular function?

ash_engineer: Also can you paste the validation error snippet from your infra? That helps us see what our runtime rejected.

devon_customer: Sending the server error: 

```
http 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": {
    "message": "Invalid arguments: expected object but got string",
    "code": "invalid_function_args"
  }
}
```

sam_sre: tagging common fixes: :white_check_mark: parse-stringified-arguments fallback, :white_check_mark: enum alias mapping layer. Both are safe as short-term patches.

maria_support: Proposed short-term workaround: when receiving a function_call where arguments is a string, attempt a best-effort parse (JSON.parse) and then run schema coercion. Also accept common enum synonyms via mapping table (e.g., {"celsius": "metric", "fahrenheit": "imperial"}). We can add that to your SDK integration as a compatibility shim.

ash_engineer: For medium-term fix: pin to a model that adheres strictly to the OpenAI calling spec (we have verified open-compat-stable-2025 performs better). Also set system prompt hints to encourage exact argument types, e.g.:

```
System: When you call functions, return arguments as JSON objects (not strings). Use enum token values exactly as defined in the schema (metric/imperial). If you are unsure, default to metric.
```

ash_engineer: Also we can add an automatic normalization step in the gateway that will: 
- detect stringified arguments and parse them,
- normalize enum values and lowercase/alias map,
- log the transformation for audit.

devon_customer: Thanks. Is there any risk of parsing something that isn't safe? We run prod workloads and are cautious about NPEs or injection.

sam_sre: Good call. Safety notes: only parse when the arguments string looks like JSON (starts with "{" or "[") and cap parse time; sanitize strings after parse; run JSON schema validation after normalization. Add a content-hash audit log for each normalization event.

maria_support: Implementation steps we suggest: 
1) Add compatibility shim in your client library (parse-stringified-args + enum alias map).
2) Enable detailed logging on the next few requests so we can confirm if model changes to object args.
3) If failures persist, we can route your account to a pinned model cluster (open-compat-stable-2025) for a test window.

devon_customer: I pasted a small patch we tried (nodejs):

```js
// compatibility shim
function normalizeFuncCall(funcCall) {
  let args = funcCall.arguments;
  if (typeof args === 'string' && args.trim().startsWith('{')) {
    try { args = JSON.parse(args); } catch(e) { return {error: 'parse_failed'}; }
  }
  // enum aliasing
  if (args && args.unit) {
    const map = { celsius: 'metric', c: 'metric', fahrenheit: 'imperial', f: 'imperial' };
    const v = (args.unit || '').toString().toLowerCase();
    if (map[v]) args.unit = map[v];
  }
  // validate with AJV (our schema lib)
  const valid = ajv.validate(funcSchema, args);
  return valid ? {ok: args} : {error: 'schema_rejected', details: ajv.errors};
}
```

devon_customer: That fixed the string parse and enum mapping locally. Still seeing occasional cases where the model uses a different function name (GetWeather vs getWeather) and our router rejects unknown functions.

maria_support: We recommend function-name normalization too (lowercase mapping) or declaring both names in your function registry temporarily. The runtime can map aliases to canonical function names.

ash_engineer: Internal note: our function registry currently is case-sensitive. We should add an alias table and a config flag to allow case-insensitive matching. Sam — want me to open an infra PR for that?

sam_sre: Please do. Low-risk change: add optional case-insensitive match with explicit opt-in per customer. We should also add telemetry so we can measure how often models deviate.

